---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GuideNav from './_GuideNav.astro';
import { guides } from './_guides.ts';

const guide = guides.find(item => item.path === 'cloudflare-pages/')!;
const headers = `https://:project.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`;
---

<Layout title={guide.title} description={guide.description}>
  <main>
    <h1>{guide.title}</h1>
    <p>{guide.description}</p>
    <p>
      The starter builds a fully static site, so Cloudflare Pages can serve it
      straight from the build output with no adapter and no server code. This
      page covers the settings that matter for search: the production URL in the
      build, previews that stay out of search results, one address per page and
      the response headers worth adding.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="settings">
      <h2 id="settings">Build settings</h2>
      <p>
        In the Cloudflare dashboard, create a Pages project and connect your
        repository. Use these settings, with your own domain in place of the
        example:
      </p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Production branch</dt>
        <dd><code>main</code></dd>
        <dt>Build command</dt>
        <dd><code>{'pnpm build --site https://example.com && pnpm verify:output && pnpm lint:html'}</code></dd>
        <dt>Build output directory</dt>
        <dd><code>dist</code></dd>
        <dt>Environment variables</dt>
        <dd><code>NODE_VERSION</code> set to <code>24.8.0</code> and <code>PNPM_VERSION</code> set to <code>12.3.4</code>, matching <code>packageManager</code> in <code>package.json</code></dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        <code>--site</code> gives the build its production origin without writing
        it into the source, which keeps a fresh fork from publishing canonical
        URLs that point at someone else's domain. If you set <code>site</code> in
        <code>astro.config.mjs</code> instead, the command can start with
        <code>pnpm build</code>. The two checks after the build read the record it
        leaves behind; if either fails, the deployment fails and the previous one
        stays live. The <a href="../build-checks/">build checks guide</a> explains
        what they look for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="previews">
      <h2 id="previews">Preview deployments</h2>
      <p>
        Every branch and pull request gets its own preview address under
        <code>pages.dev</code>. Cloudflare already sends
        <code>X-Robots-Tag: noindex</code> for requests to preview addresses. To
        mark the pages themselves as well, add <code>SITE_NOINDEX</code> with the
        value <code>true</code> to the preview environment's variables and leave
        it unset for production. Keep the production origin in the preview build
        command: canonical URLs then point at the real site, so a preview that
        does get crawled credits production rather than competing with it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="domain">
      <h2 id="domain">A custom domain, and one address per page</h2>
      <p>
        Add your domain under the project's custom domains; the
        <code>site</code> value only controls generated URLs and does not touch
        DNS. Once the domain works, the same pages are still reachable at
        <code>your-project.pages.dev</code>. The canonical links already name
        your domain, but a redirect is cleaner. Cloudflare does this with Bulk
        Redirects: create a list with <code>your-project.pages.dev</code> as the
        source, <code>https://example.com</code> as the target and status
        <code>301</code>, tick preserve query string, subpath matching and
        preserve path suffix, then create a Bulk Redirect rule that uses the
        list. Include subdomains also redirects the preview addresses under
        <code>your-project.pages.dev</code>, so leave it unticked if you review
        previews there.
      </p>
      <p>
        Within your domain, Pages serves <code>about/index.html</code> at
        <code>/about/</code> and redirects both <code>/about</code> and
        <code>/about/index.html</code> there. That matches the starter's
        <code>trailingSlash: 'always'</code>, so canonical links, the sitemap and
        the served URL are the same address.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="not-found">
      <h2 id="not-found">The 404 page</h2>
      <p>
        Keep <code>src/pages/404.astro</code>. Pages serves a top-level
        <code>404.html</code> with a real 404 status for unknown paths. Without
        one, Pages treats the project as a single-page application and answers
        every unknown path with the homepage and a 200 status, which search
        engines report as soft 404s.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="headers">
      <h2 id="headers">Response headers</h2>
      <p>
        Pages sends <code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate</code>
        with an <code>ETag</code> by default, so browsers revalidate each file.
        That suits the starter: title cards, icons and pages keep the same URL
        when their content changes. To add headers, create
        <code>public/_headers</code>; Astro copies it into the output. This
        example keeps the <code>pages.dev</code> address out of search results
        if you skip the redirect, and caches the fingerprinted files Astro writes
        to <code>/_astro/</code> once you add styles or scripts:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{headers}</code></pre>
      <p>
        A <code>_headers</code> file takes up to 100 rules with lines of up to
        2,000 characters. It applies to static files only, not to responses from
        Pages Functions. Do not mark title cards or icons as immutable; they
        would stay stale in caches after you change them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="after">
      <h2 id="after">After the first deployment</h2>
      <p>
        Open <code>/robots.txt</code> and <code>/sitemap-index.xml</code> on your
        domain and check that they name it. View the source of a page and check its
        canonical link. Then add the domain to Google Search Console and Bing
        Webmaster Tools and submit the sitemap index in both.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideNav current={guide.path} />
  </main>
</Layout>
